A||||4||Ferrari_cdn||Sticky throttle cable||||1||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||09-26-2001||03:58 AM||||At least I think I do.  When I am driving and press the clutch, say at 2000, my rpms sometimes likes to go to 3000 and SLOWLY decline.  When I go under the gas pedal with my shoe and pull the pedal towards me, my rpms suddenly drops to 900.  Another example is when Im driving at 70 kph and in 4th, Im not giving it any gas, I notice she will like to stay at that speed.  I pull the pedal again, it'll start declining in speed slowly.<P>As a forum member told me, it should be from a sticky throttle cable or something else I don't remember.  I should open the air filter and then remove two more nuts inside and carefully move whatever THAT is (I forgot what it is) and oil the moving parts under because it could be sticky and keeping the throttle in when it's already released.  Is there anything else I should be on the lookout for and fix/oil?  I hate having high idle when I am in neutral or have the clutch disengaged.  My tank goes dry at 225 kilometres!  My friend's V6 empties at 250.  I should go longer than that.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_2.JPG">  <BR>'84 2m4, 4 speed, 106k miles (157k km)  AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000001||2farnorth||09-26-2001||06:17 AM||||Check both throttle return springs at the throttle body:<BR> 1 small one around the end of the butterfly shaft.<BR> 2. the other is right up on top and hooks to the throttle linkage and the bracket that the cable clips into.<P>Also check the cable where it goes by the exhaust manifold and make sure it hasn't melted.  If it has melted it should be replaced (Not easy)<P>Lube all the external moving parts of the throttle linkage from the end of the cable to the butterfly shaft in the throttle body.<P>Good luck.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ferrari_cdn||09-26-2001||03:46 PM||||*bump*||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-26-2001||07:38 PM||||check over the pedal hardware carefully. Make sure nothing is catching on the top end of the thing. The fact that you can get the idle down by pulling up the pedal is odd. It tends to point to something dragging or actual damage to the pedal. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||09-27-2001||01:19 AM||||Ogre: I've looked at the thing. IMHO, the return spring on the TBI side of things is very floppy. After he taps the gas, if you flick the linkage right at the throttle, it'll settle at Idle. Either something's a little stiff, or that spring has to go... <BR>Pedal seems fine. I just told him to oil everything that moves, and evaluate there. That way if I'm wrong, all he does is blow $0.34 worth of WD-40  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>I told him to take apart the air cleaner for better access to the moving bits, and so he could see if there's anything VISIBLY wrong (like twisted wires, frayed edges, dead mice gunking up the works  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )...<P>Oh, crap... Ferrari: Did I TELL you to be on the lookout? I'm pretty SURE I did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||10
